├─ List usage (ordered/unordered)
├─ Schema markup (JSON-LD present?)
├─ Author info (byline, credentials)
├─ Publication date & freshness
├─ Internal link anchors
├─ Image alt text quality
├─ Citation depth (number of external links)
└─ E-E-A-T signals (credentials, about page reference)

Step 2: Scoring Against AI Preferences
Each AI model has DIFFERENT preferences:

ChatGPT Preferences:
├─ Clear, conversational structure
├─ Well-formatted lists
├─ Strong heading hierarchy
└─ Publication date signals

Claude Preferences:
├─ Nuanced, balanced arguments
├─ Source citations
├─ Footnote references
└─ Logical flow

Gemini Preferences:
├─ Schema markup (critical)
├─ Mobile-friendly formatting
├─ Quick answer format
└─ Fact boxes/tables

Perplexity Preferences:
├─ Multi-source citations
├─ Clear data/statistics
├─ Expert attribution
└─ Topic depth
Each page gets scored 0-100 for EACH model based on presence of these signals.


WHAT THEY'RE NOT DOING (Important Distinction)
❌ They're NOT querying ChatGPT API to check if you're cited
- This would be expensive and unreliable
- API rate limits would prevent consistent checking
- Results would vary session to session

✅ They're analyzing local page signals that AI models prefer

- Downloaded once, analyzed locally
- Consistent, predictable scoring
- Scalable to unlimited users

